{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>If you do this thing, etc., and Elohim commands you, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of his saying <b>and He commands you</b>: if he is speaking of prevailing in gevurah, he already said this to him once, as it is written (Exodus 18:19): ״I will advise you, and may Elohim be with you״ — and what need is there to repeat the matter? Perhaps, however, he intended to give him an answer to what he might claim, for in the end he is depriving himself of the mitzvot, and it is not the way of the righteous to lighten their burden of mitzvot, even though he grows faint and even though he grows weary, for man is born to toil (Job 5:7) — in the toil of a mitzvah. Therefore he said to him, <b>and Elohim commands you</b>, etc. — meaning, for behold, it is known what is the extremity of strength that a prophet requires; go out and learn from the words of Daniel (Daniel 10:7) when an angel spoke to him, and from this you will gain understanding regarding dibburot of El. And therefore you will find that they said, their memory for blessing (Nedarim 38a): prophecy rests only upon a gibbor. And it is true that Moshe purified his body and the weakening of strength would not befall him, but in any event he could not fortify himself so completely when he would be weak in his nature. And therefore he said to him that it is more fitting in this mitzvah, which can be fulfilled through others, so that he may fulfill the mitzvah of prophecy, which cannot be fulfilled through anyone but him. And that is what he says — <b>if you do this</b>, etc., <b>and Elohim commands you</b> — meaning, when Elohim commands you the mitzvot that He commands you in the giving of the Torah and the mitzvah, you will be able to stand in them, meaning there will be strength in you to stand in them. Or perhaps he was precise in using the language of standing to say that there will be in him strength to receive prophecy while standing, as they said, their memory for blessing (Zohar I:171a), that the other prophets would fall on their faces, whereas Moshe, as it is written, ״and you shall stand there״, etc. (Exodus 34:2), ״stand here with Me״ (Deuteronomy 5:28). And therefore if he does not lighten this burden from upon himself, it will be a cause that he will diminish, heaven forbid, from the level of a righteous person, and he will not be able to stand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:46.545259756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}